title: The SPD votes for rule by decree
subtitle: The Reichstag passes an Enabling Act for Stresemann.
new-page: true
tags: kpd_event
view-if: year = 1923 and month = 10 and y23_enabling_done = 0 and civil_war = 0
on-arrival: y23_enabling_done = 1

= The Enabling Act

Stresemann's second cabinet asks the Reichstag for the power to rule by decree on "financial, economic and social" matters. It needs two thirds. Everyone knows the first decrees will touch the eight-hour day, the one thing the revolution of 1918 won and the workers still hold. On the first attempt the act fails for want of a quorum, because the German Nationals and we walk out. On the 13th it comes back.

{!<div class="kpd-insert" data-insert="vote" data-arg="enabling"></div>!}

The SPD's deputies vote for it. Some of them say it is the price of keeping the Republic; some say nothing at all.

- @against: Vote against, and tell the SPD workers what their deputies voted for.
- @silent: Vote against, but keep the attack on the SPD quiet while Saxony is at stake.

@against
subtitle: Our vote among the workers rises. Joint action with the SPD suffers. · dev: workers_kpd +3×m, workers_spd −2, y23_unity −1, kf_left_strength +2
on-arrival: workers_kpd += 3*campaign_mult; workers_spd -= 2; y23_unity -= 1; kf_left_strength += 2
go-to: root

Our deputies vote no, and the next morning Die Rote Fahne prints the names of the SPD deputies who voted to give the cabinet the eight-hour day. It is true, and the workers read it. The SPD left in Saxony, which needs its own party's tolerance in Berlin, reads it too.

@silent
subtitle: The alliance in Saxony holds. The Left calls it cowardice. · dev: kf_left_dissent +5, trust −1
on-arrival: kf_left_dissent += 5; comintern_trust -= 1
go-to: root

Our deputies vote no and say little. The paper reports the vote on page two. Zeigner's people are grateful, and the party's Left asks whether a workers' government in Dresden is worth the eight-hour day in the whole Reich.
